A||N||5||mbramble||85GT Questions||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||03-25-2001||11:48 AM||||I just purchased and '85 GT for a project car.  There are two fan blower looking assemblies in the rear trunk -- one mounted to the right side of the trunk and covered by the carpet, the other mounted in the bottom of the trunk and mounted to the rear wall of the trunk.  What are these for??  Also, there are two tubes which are mounted to the front side of the trunk wall, in the engine compartment.  They appear to be an inch or so in diameter and both have been cut off.  What are/were these for??<P>Thanks much.  My other car is an '87 Duke Coupe and all this looks might different!!<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||03-25-2001||12:00 PM||||I don't think I can help you on the fan things, but I believe those cutoff 1" tubes are for cooling.  I think one cools the coil?  If you have the engine running, you can barely feel air coming out of them.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||03-25-2001||12:03 PM||||These blowers are attached to the tubes you mentioned, and provide supposedly *cool* air to the alternator and the coil. I can't say as a fact, but I've read that they come on with the radiator fan. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||03-25-2001||12:18 PM||||I guess you're right.  My fan doesn't come on; blown switch, but it does come on with the fan.  Let the engine run and turn on the A/C, THEN see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||03-25-2001||01:57 PM||||It is correct about the fan covered with carpet blowing air to the coil and alternator. Once you turn it on and put your hand in front of the tubes you will find that it does blow significant air (okay, at least mine does)! It would be a good idea to replace them if they have been cut off. Sitting in traffic can get the engine bay pretty hot! 88 V6s didn't use the fans because the crossover pipe was insulated to keep the temps down. Unfortunately 88s are the only ones that occasionally have problems with cracked crossover pipes.<P>You can activate the fans simply by turning the key to turn on the electrical systems and then push the AC button. The radiator fan will turn on and the engine fans will turn on.<P>I have an 85 GT and are puzzled by this second fan on the bottom of the trunk. I don't have this on mine. The muffler is on the other side of the trunk and it might be an attempt to "cool" off the muffler (???). It is NOT stock and I've never heard anyone having problems with heating up the trunk unless there was a hole in the muffler. Then things get hot!<P>Hope this helps!||||reg||1
Z||000005||mbramble||03-25-2001||02:09 PM||||WKDFIRO,<P>Yes it does help.  I notice now that the two tubes point to the alternator and the coil.<P>The 2nd fan, mounted down in the bottom of the cargo area undoubtedly has something to do with circulating air out of the area because the previous owner had moved the battery down there and actually had a dual battery system.  He had built a shelf to cover the batterys and must have been trying to keep battery gases from building up in the area.  Just one of many mods that I will be undoing!!<P>Thanks much,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
